---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

// PROOF'S TERMS OF USE. The text below is NOT authored here. Its source of truth is
// proof/docs/TERMS-OF-USE.md in the Proof repo, where it lives next to the code it describes, so
// that a change to what the app does and a change to what this page claims can land in one commit.
// That is the same arrangement chain/privacy.astro and caliper/privacy.astro use, and the reason
// is the same: correcting the copy instead of the source is how a page and an app drift apart.
// If something here looks wrong, fix docs/TERMS-OF-USE.md and re-render.
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. Apple's Developer Program License Agreement, Schedule 2 section 3.8(b),
// v126: "Links to Your Privacy Policy and Terms of Use must be accessible within Your Licensed
// Application." Proof ships auto-renewable subscriptions, so it cannot be submitted without a
// Terms of Use at a live URL that the app itself links to.
//
// IT MUST NOT POINT AT /terms/. That page is the PLUGIN line's terms and every load-bearing
// sentence in it is false for Proof: it names FastSpring as the seller (Apple is), it describes a
// .wllicense file (App Review guideline 3.1.1 forbids one), it says there is nothing to cancel
// (Proof is primarily a subscription), it promises 14 days no questions asked (not ours to
// promise on Apple's behalf), and it calls the line a public beta (Proof ships at 1.0.0).
// Handing an App Review reviewer that document from inside Proof would hand them a contradiction
// of the purchase they are reviewing. Hence a per-product path, /proof/terms/, which also scales
// when the third product needs one.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DRAFT, AND WHAT THE FLAG BELOW CONTROLS.
//
// DRAFT=true does three things at once, deliberately, so they cannot come apart:
//   1. renders the banner at the top of the page,
//   2. sets `noindex` on the Layout, which also drops the canonical link,
//   3. is PAIRED with an entry in the sitemap filter in astro.config.mjs.
// (2) and (3) are a pair the way /canjam and /card are: a noindexed page listed in the sitemap
// asks a crawler to fetch it and then tells it to forget what it found. astro.config.mjs cannot
// read this flag, so the filter carries a comment naming this line. CHANGE BOTH OR NEITHER.
//
// WHAT HAS TO HAPPEN BEFORE DRAFT GOES FALSE:
//   a. `updatesWindowStart` below stops being null — see the note on it. NEEDS JAMEY.
//   b. A lawyer has read it. docs/TERMS-OF-USE.md section 16 lists the eight specific questions,
//      in priority order, rather than "please review this".
//   c. Jamey decides the standard-EULA question in docs/TERMS-OF-USE.md section 15 and fills in
//      the License Agreement field in App Store Connect to match.
//   d. `updated` below becomes the date of publication.
// Only after the URL returns 200 does the in-app link get wired — docs/STOREKIT.md section 7.6
// names the place. A Terms of Use link inside the app that 404s is worse than no link, because it
// is a broken promise a reviewer will click.
//
// NOT REVIEWED BY A LAWYER. Every factual claim in it was checked against the Proof repo on
// 2026-09-18: the free/paid seam against docs/FREE-TIER.md section 9, the three ways to pay and
// the one-week trial against docs/DESIGN.md section 16.2a, and the "no data collected, no
// tracking" claim against resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy (NSPrivacyTracking false,
// NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes an empty array). Whether the document is *sufficient* as a contract
// is exactly what (b) above is for.
//
// TWO SENTENCES THAT ARE DELIBERATELY NARROW. Do not "tidy" either one:
//   * The section on what Proof's numbers are for disclaims regulatory, safety and compliance use
//     while explicitly NOT disclaiming the product's own claim. Proof's whole thesis is that its
//     numbers are honest about their own uncertainty; terms reading "these numbers mean nothing"
//     would contradict the thing being sold. The wording draws a line between HONESTY and
//     AUTHORITY, and both halves are load-bearing.
//   * The data section names the two connections the app does make rather than claiming it makes
//     none. "Proof makes no network connections" was in the first draft and is false: a
//     subscription app talks to the App Store, and this very page is a web address the app opens.
//     The same absolute has already been retracted once in resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy.
const DRAFT = true;

// When the twelve months of updates that come with the one-time purchase start running.
//
// NULL ON PURPOSE, AND THIS IS THE ONE THING THAT BLOCKS PUBLICATION. docs/DESIGN.md section 16.2a
// owns the promise and says only "current major version plus 12 months of updates". It does not
// say whether the twelve months run from the date of purchase or from the release of the version
// bought. A published legal document is the worst possible place to invent the missing half of
// somebody else's fact, so the gap is rendered visibly instead of filled in. **NEEDS JAMEY**, and
// it has to be fixed at the owner — docs/DESIGN.md — before it is copied down to here.
//
// Set it to a phrase that completes "counted ___", e.g. 'from the day you buy it' or 'from the
// day the version you bought was released'.
const updatesWindowStart = null;

// The guard, rather than a comment asking nicely. Astro evaluates frontmatter at build time, so
// this fails `npm run build` loudly instead of letting a finished-looking page ship with a hole in
// a promise. The site's own documented failure mode is the silent one: a page that builds, pushes
// and is simply wrong at the URL, with no error anywhere.
if (!DRAFT && updatesWindowStart === null) {
  throw new Error(
    'proof/terms.astro: DRAFT is false but updatesWindowStart is still null. ' +
      'docs/DESIGN.md section 16.2a has to say when the perpetual twelve months start before ' +
      'this page can be published. See the note above updatesWindowStart.'
  );
}

// While DRAFT, this is the drafting date and the page says so. On publication it becomes the
// publication date and the footer reads "Last updated".
const updated = '18 September 2026';
const email = 'jamey@warrenlabs.com';
---

<Layout
  title="Terms of Use · Proof · Warren Labs"
  description="The terms for buying and using Proof. Apple is the seller, there is one tier with three ways to pay, and everything you measure stays on your device."
  noindex={DRAFT}
>
  <Nav variant="home" />
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Proof</span>
      <h2>Terms of Use</h2>
      <p class="byline">
        <b>Proof</b>, by Warren Labs.
        {DRAFT ? <Fragment>Drafted {updated}. Not yet in force.</Fragment> : <Fragment>Last updated {updated}.</Fragment>}
      </p>

      {DRAFT && (
        <div class="draftbar">
          <b>This is a draft. It is not in force and it is not the terms of any sale.</b>
          <span>
            It has not been reviewed by a lawyer, and one question in it is still open — it is
            marked, below, where it belongs. Nothing on this page binds anybody until that is
            settled and this notice is gone.
          </span>
        </div>
      )}

      <p class="sec-lede">
        You are buying a licence to use Proof. <b>Apple is the seller</b> and handles the money.
        <b> Everything you measure stays on your device.</b> The rest of this page is the detail
        behind those three sentences, written to be read rather than skimmed past.
      </p>

      <h3>Who you are buying from</h3>
      <p>
        Proof is made by <b>Warren Labs</b>, a trade name of <b>Free River Studios LLC</b>, a
        Montana limited liability company operating from Livingston, Montana, United States. "We"
        and "us" below mean Free River Studios LLC.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Apple is the seller.</b> Proof is sold through the App Store, and Apple handles the
        payment, the tax, the receipt and the refund. Your contract for the transaction is with
        Apple and is governed by Apple's terms, which you accepted when you set up your Apple
        Account. This page covers the software itself, and the relationship between you and us.
        Both relationships are real and they cover different things.
      </p>
      <p>
        These terms are Proof's. The Warren Labs audio plugins are sold somewhere else entirely,
        under <a href="/terms/">their own terms</a>, and that page does not apply to anything you
        buy in the App Store.
      </p>

      <h3>What you are buying</h3>
      <p>
        A <b>licence to use the software</b>, not the software itself and not its source code.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is <b>one tier</b>. Everything Proof can do is in it. There is no professional
        edition, no add-on and no feature you can unlock separately, and we do not intend to add
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are three ways to pay for that one tier: a monthly subscription, a yearly
        subscription, or a single purchase that does not renew. Prices are shown in the app and on
        the App Store, and they are not repeated here — a price written in two places is a price
        that will eventually disagree with itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What the licence lets you do.</b> Use Proof on the devices associated with your Apple
        Account, as Apple's own terms allow, including for paid commercial work, with no royalty,
        no extra licence and no reporting. Proof is a <b>Universal Purchase</b>: one purchase
        covers the Mac, iPad and iPhone versions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What it does not let you do.</b> Resell, rent, redistribute or sublicense it.
        Reverse-engineer, decompile, or take it apart for use in another product.
      </p>

      <h3>What is free, and what is paid</h3>
      <p>
        Proof has a free half that needs no purchase and no account, and it is not a trial or a
        teaser.
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <b>Free, permanently:</b> the live instruments — the real-time analysers, the meters and
          the signal generator — and <b>opening, checking and reading any Proof measurement
          record</b>, including one somebody else made and sent you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Paid:</b> taking a measurement — the sweep, the noise-floor measurement it is held
          against, the record that comes out of it, the library of records, comparing them, and
          export.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your subscription ends, <b>you do not lose anything you measured</b>. You keep read
        access to the records you have already written, along with everything in the free half. You
        cannot take new measurements until you subscribe again. We do not hold your data hostage,
        and there is no state of this app in which a measurement you paid to make becomes
        unreadable.
      </p>

      <h3>The subscription, and how to cancel it</h3>
      <p>
        A subscription <b>renews automatically</b> at the end of each period — every month for the
        monthly one, every year for the yearly one — and is charged to your Apple Account until you
        cancel it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Apple owns cancellation, and we cannot do it for you.</b> Cancel in
        <b> Settings &rsaquo; your name &rsaquo; Subscriptions</b> on an iPhone or iPad, or
        <b> App Store &rsaquo; your account &rsaquo; Account Settings &rsaquo; Subscriptions</b> on
        a Mac. The change takes effect at the end of the period you have already paid for; you keep
        what you bought until then. Apple requires cancellation <b>at least 24 hours before</b> a
        period ends for it to stop the next charge.
      </p>
      <p>
        We cannot see your subscription, cannot cancel it, and cannot restart it. If you ask us to,
        the only honest answer we have is this paragraph.
      </p>

      <h3>The free trial</h3>
      <p>
        The subscriptions carry a <b>one-week free trial</b>, offered through Apple.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three things about it you should know before it starts, because they are Apple's rules
        rather than ours and we would rather you met them here than at a charge you did not expect:
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <b>When the week ends you are charged the full price of the subscription you chose</b>,
          automatically, unless you cancel first. Cancel at least 24 hours before the week is up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>There is one trial per subscription group, not one per product.</b> If you trial the
          monthly and then switch to the yearly, there is no second trial; the yearly is billed in
          full. The app will not offer you a trial you are not eligible for.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Buying Proof outright carries no trial.</b> Apple's introductory offers exist only for
          subscriptions that renew, and no wording of ours can change that.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There is no countdown in the app, no "trial expired" screen, and no nag. Apple owns the
        clock. The app only ever asks whether it is entitled right now.
      </p>

      <h3>Buying it outright</h3>
      <p>
        The single purchase does not renew, there is nothing to cancel, and it does not expire. It
        covers <b>the current major version of Proof, plus twelve months of updates</b>{
          updatesWindowStart ? <Fragment>, counted {updatesWindowStart}</Fragment> : null
        }. After that the app you own keeps working, exactly as it is, for as long as your hardware
        and operating system will run it. It simply stops receiving new versions unless you buy the
        next major one.
      </p>

      {/* THE OPEN QUESTION. Rendered rather than quietly resolved, because docs/DESIGN.md section
          16.2a — which owns the promise — does not say when the twelve months start, and this page
          is not allowed to pick. See the note above `updatesWindowStart`. NEEDS JAMEY. */}
      {updatesWindowStart === null && (
        <p class="gap">
          <b>One thing here is not settled yet, and we are not going to guess at it in a legal
          document.</b> Whether those twelve months run from the day you buy Proof, or from the day
          the version you bought was released, has not been decided. It will say so here, plainly,
          before this page is in force.
        </p>
      )}

      <p>
        <b>There is no upgrade path from a subscription to buying it outright.</b> Apple offers
        upgrades and credit only between subscriptions inside one group, and a single purchase is
        in no group. If you have been subscribing and decide you would rather own it, you pay the
        full price and nothing is credited. We would rather tell you that here than have you find
        it out at the till. If that lands badly for you, write to us — any goodwill we extend is
        our own bookkeeping, and we would rather do it than have you feel tricked.
      </p>

      <h3>Refunds</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Apple issues refunds for App Store purchases, and we cannot.</b> We never see the
        transaction, there is no order number on our side to look up, and Apple gives developers no
        way to refund one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ask Apple at <b>reportaproblem.apple.com</b>, signed in with the Apple Account you bought
        with. Choose the purchase, pick a reason, submit. Apple usually decides within a couple of
        days.
      </p>
      <p>
        An App Store refund is Apple's decision, under Apple's terms and under whatever consumer
        law applies where you live.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are in the UK or the EU, Apple publishes terms for your region, and they are what
        govern this. The two we read — the UK's and Ireland's — each give you a
        <b> fourteen-day right to withdraw</b> from an order, exercised through Apple and not
        through us, and each states an exception to it:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          "You cannot cancel your order for the supply of Content if the delivery has started upon
          your request and acknowledgement that you thereby lose your right of withdrawal."
        </p>
        <cite>
          Apple Media Services Terms and Conditions (Ireland), read 18 September 2026. Apple's UK
          page carries the same sentence, calling the same thing your cancellation right.
        </cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Whether installing Proof starts delivery in that sense is Apple's call and the law's, not
        ours, and we are not going to tell you the answer we would prefer. Ask Apple. And
        <a href="#statutory">your statutory rights</a> below stand whatever the answer is: nothing
        on this page takes away a right the law gives you.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Apple turns you down and you think that is wrong, write to us anyway. We cannot overturn
        their decision and we are not going to pretend otherwise — but if Proof did not do what we
        said it does, we want to know, and that is worth more to us than the sale.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our general refund policy is at <a href="/refunds/">warrenlabs.com/refunds</a>, which
        already explains this distinction for another App Store product.
      </p>

      <h3>What Proof's numbers mean, and what they are not for</h3>
      <p>
        <b>This section is the boundary of what Proof claims. It is not a retraction of it.</b>
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What Proof claims, and we stand behind it.</b> Every reading Proof prints is reported
        against a noise-and-distortion floor that the instrument measured on the equipment in front
        of it. A reading that is clear of that floor is printed with the number of digits the floor
        entitles it to. A reading at or under the floor is printed as an inequality with no digits,
        because the honest statement is "quieter than this instrument can hear". Most analysers
        print a number there. Proof does not, and that refusal is the product. Nothing on this page
        is an admission that Proof's numbers are vague, unreliable or not to be believed. They are
        stated with their uncertainty attached, which is more than most tools in this class do.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What that does not make Proof.</b> It is not an accredited, certified or traceable
        instrument. We provide no calibration certificate and no traceability to a national
        standard, and we hold no accreditation of any kind. Proof measures through hardware we did
        not make, in a room we have not seen, operated by someone we cannot supervise. Where the
        app supports calibration — sound pressure level can be calibrated against an acoustic
        calibrator you supply — the accuracy of that calibration is the accuracy of your calibrator
        and your microphone, not something we can warrant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>So, plainly: do not use Proof as the instrument of record where the number itself
        carries legal, regulatory or safety weight.</b> That includes, and is not limited to:
      </p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          workplace or environmental noise-exposure compliance, and any measurement a regulator
          will act on;
        </li>
        <li>product safety certification, type approval, or conformity assessment;</li>
        <li>building-code or environmental-health acoustic compliance;</li>
        <li>medical, audiological or hearing-health decisions of any kind;</li>
        <li>
          evidence in a dispute where the measurement itself is the thing being argued about.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Those uses need an instrument with documented calibration and traceability, usually to a
        defined class or type, operated to a published method. Proof is not one and does not claim
        to be. The sound pressure level meter and the noise dosimeter report a measured quantity
        and prescribe nothing; they are not a substitute for a class- or type-rated sound level
        meter where one is required.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>About the records specifically.</b> A Proof record carries a content hash. That hash
        proves the document has not been altered since it was written, and anyone can check it
        without our software. It does <b>not</b> prove the equipment was set up correctly, that the
        right thing was measured, or that the person holding it is who they say they are — and the
        format makes no such claim. A record is a statement about what this instrument measured, on
        this equipment, at that moment. Treat it as exactly that.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The distinction we are drawing is between honesty and authority.</b> Proof is honest
        about its own uncertainty. It is not an authority, and no amount of honesty makes it one.
      </p>

      <h3>Your records are yours</h3>
      <p>
        A Proof record is <b>your data</b>. You made it. These terms give us no claim on it of any
        kind.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Nothing you measure leaves your device.</b> Proof has <b>no account, no server of ours,
        no analytics, no crash reporting and no telemetry</b>. Nothing you measure, and nothing
        about how you use the app, is sent to us or to anyone else, because there is nowhere for it
        to be sent: we operate no service for Proof to talk to. If you want a record to go
        somewhere, you export it and send it yourself.
      </p>
      <p>There are two things the app reaches out for, and neither carries anything you measured:</p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <b>The App Store</b>, to show you prices and to complete a purchase or a restore. That is
          Apple's connection, on Apple's terms, and the transaction is between you and Apple.
          Nothing about your measurements is in it. Checking whether you are entitled does not need
          it: that reads a signed record Apple has already put on your device, which is why the app
          keeps working on a bench with no internet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A link you tap.</b> Where the app offers a link to a web page — this document, or our
          privacy policy — tapping it opens your browser. The app opens nothing you did not ask it
          to.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The privacy manifest that ships inside the app declares <b>no data collected and no
        tracking</b>, and our App Store privacy answer is <b>Data Not Collected</b>. This page and
        that manifest have to agree. If they ever disagree, the manifest is what is actually
        running and this page is the thing that is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        You may use, publish, sell, keep or destroy your records as you like. You need no
        permission from us, you owe us no attribution, and there is no licence back to us hiding
        anywhere in this document. The record format is documented and the rule for checking one is
        plain text, deliberately, so that a record can be checked with a hash function and none of
        our software at all — including in a future where we no longer exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>We cannot recover a record you lose. We never had it.</b> Back up your device.
      </p>

      <h3>Updates, and changes to the app</h3>
      <p>
        Updates within the version you bought are included. We may add, change or remove features
        in an update, and we will not pretend otherwise here so that we can quietly change our
        minds later.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we will not do is take away the thing you paid for. If Proof's business model ever
        changes, a licence already sold keeps working for the version it bought, and existing
        customers will be told directly rather than by us editing this page.
      </p>

      <h3>What we don't promise</h3>
      <p>
        Proof is provided <b>as it is</b>. We do not warrant that it is free of defects, that it is
        fit for any particular purpose, that its results are accurate for your equipment, or that
        it will run uninterrupted. No software vendor can honestly promise those things, and a
        measurement tool least of all.
      </p>
      <p>
        That paragraph is a legal backstop, not a shrug. If Proof gives you a wrong answer, tell
        us. We would rather fix the instrument than point at this section — an instrument whose
        maker is not interested in its errors is not an instrument.
      </p>

      <h3>The limit on what we owe you</h3>
      <p>
        To the fullest extent the law allows, our total liability to you arising out of or relating
        to Proof is limited to the amount you have paid for Proof.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not liable for indirect, incidental, special or consequential loss, including lost
        profits, lost work, lost data, or loss arising from a decision you made on the basis of a
        measurement. The section above on what Proof's numbers are for is the reason that last
        clause is there, and it is why that section is written as plainly as it is.
      </p>

      <h3 id="statutory">Your statutory rights</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing in these terms removes rights the law gives you. If you are a consumer in the
        European Union, the United Kingdom, or anywhere else with mandatory consumer protection,
        those rights apply on top of this page and override anything here that conflicts with them.
      </p>

      <h3>Changes to these terms</h3>
      <p>
        If we change this page, the change applies to purchases made <b>after</b> the change. It
        does not reach backwards into a licence you already hold. The date at the bottom is when it
        was last touched.
      </p>

      <h3>Governing law</h3>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the law of the State of Montana, United States, without regard
        to its conflict-of-law rules. Your separate purchase contract with Apple is governed by
        Apple's terms.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> — anything at all, including the awkward questions.
        A real person reads it.
      </p>

      <p class="meta">
        {DRAFT ? <Fragment>Drafted {updated} &middot; not in force</Fragment> : <Fragment>Last updated {updated}</Fragment>}
        &middot; <a href="/proof/">Proof</a>
        &middot; <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
        &middot; <a href="/refunds/">Refunds</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Follows terms.astro and chain/privacy.astro. .prose, .points and .byline are page-local
     classes on this site rather than global ones, so a legal page that does not carry this block
     renders with no measure and an unstyled list. */
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h3 { margin: 34px 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .prose p { margin: 0 0 14px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose b { color: var(--text); }
  .prose a { color: var(--accent); }
  .byline { font-size: 0.92rem; opacity: 0.6; margin-bottom: 18px; }
  .points { margin: 0 0 14px; padding-left: 20px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .points li { margin: 0 0 8px; }

  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  blockquote p { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; }
  blockquote cite {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--muted-2);
  }

  /* The draft notice. Amber is the line's signal colour and this is the one thing on the page
     that is a signal: the document is not in force. It disappears with the DRAFT flag. */
  .draftbar {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-line);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .draftbar b { display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text); }
  .draftbar span { display: block; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--muted); }

  /* The one unanswered question, rendered where it belongs rather than resolved by guesswork. */
  .gap {
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .prose .meta {
    margin-top: 34px;
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted-2);
  }
</style>
